// Hand-written rule cards that REPLACE the generated ones.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `netlify/functions/_shared/ruleCards.mjs` is generated by
// `scripts/build-lesson-pool.mjs` from `grammar-content-cache.json` (dumpedAt
// 2026-08-24 — i.e. from before the A1.1 rebuild) and must not be hand-edited.
// The second DaF review found four of those cards wrong for the rebuilt course
// (docs/course-factory/a11-rebuild/REVIEW-daf-2-2026-09-12.md §D, fix list #10)
// and the card text is what `netlify/functions/explain-answer.mjs` puts into the
// system prompt behind "Erklär mir das" — the place a learner goes when the
// Lektion did not carry them. Fixing the cards at the source means editing
// `grammar_rules` in Supabase and re-dumping the cache; this file is the
// repo-side fix that does not wait for that, and it is diffable and testable.
//
// HOW IT IS APPLIED. `scripts/build-lesson-pool.mjs` imports this module
// optionally and merges the default export OVER the generated cards, keyed by
// slug. A card here therefore wins whole — it is not a patch, it replaces the
// generated object — so every override must carry the SAME KEYS as the
// generated card it replaces (`titleDe`, `content`, `commonMistakes`, and each
// mistake's `wrong`/`correct`/`explanationDe`). `tests/rule-card-overrides.test.mjs`
// pins that against the committed `ruleCards.mjs`.
//
// THE FOUR FINDINGS THIS ANSWERS:
//   * `alphabet-pronunciation` (MAJOR) — the generated card is an
//     English-contrastive pronunciation table with not one German letter NAME,
//     in the free lesson, whose items ask for exactly those names ("Wie heißt
//     der Buchstabe Z?"). Replaced by the letter names plus how to spell a name
//     aloud, with examples from the Lektion 1/2 Wortfeld.
//   * `time-and-dates` (MAJOR) — the generated card is titled
//     "Wiederholung: Zahlen 1–12" and is a number table; the clock appears only
//     in `commonMistakes`, and so does the ordinal-date rule ("am fünften Mai")
//     that the course defers to A1.2 in writing. Replaced by the clock itself
//     (official vs. everyday) and um/am/im, with the date explicitly deferred.
//   * `nouns-gender` (MINOR) — "~40 % · ~35 % · ~25 %" with no source, against
//     the repo's measure-before-you-claim rule. Replaced by the gender-predicting
//     endings, which are teachable and need no count.
//   * `yes-no-questions` (MINOR) — card and pool contradicted each other on the
//     spoken intonation question. `commonMistakes[0]` now names the written rule
//     ("Verb auf Platz 1") and says the spoken form exists.
//
// STYLE RULE FOR THIS FILE. Controlled A1 German, one short English support
// line per card (the generated cards were half English; L1 support is defensible
// for A1, but then deliberately and everywhere). No sound comparisons to
// English, no percentages, no ordinal numbers on the time card.

